[E-TYPE-MISMATCH] Error: type mismatch
     ╭─[ reject/parser-count-differs-from-lemma.sw:11:3 ]
     │
  11 │   Parse.readsExactly 36 Parse.digit DigitWritten Parse.readsDigit
     │   ───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────  
     │                                  ╰───────────────────────────────── expected `∀ (state: Parse.State) (value: List (Fin 10)) (after: Parse.Mark) => state.at.left = state.at.rest.length → Parse.runFrom state (arg => Std.Effect.bind … …) = Ok (value, after) → ∃ (read: List Char) => ….rest = read ++ … ∧ … = … ∧ … ∧ …`, but this has type `Parse.Reads (arg => Parse.exactly 36 Parse.digit) (read values => Parse.Each DigitWritten read values ∧ values.length = 36)`
     │
     ├─[ std/Parse/Parse.sw:154:14 ]
     │
 155 │ ╭─▶         match state.at.rest with
     ┆ ┆   
 157 │ ├─▶           | [] => k None state
     │ │                                    
     │ ╰──────────────────────────────────── this `match`
     │     
     │     Note 1: `Parse.Reads (arg => Parse.exactly 36 Parse.digit) (read values => Parse.Each DigitWritten read values ∧ values.length = 36)` is `∀ (state: Parse.State) (value: List (Fin 10)) (after: Parse.Mark) => state.at.left = state.at.rest.length → (match state.at.rest with …) = Ok (value, after) → ∃ (read: List Char) => ….rest = read ++ … ∧ … = … ∧ … ∧ …`
     │     
     │     Note 2: `(match …)` is a `match` written inside `Parse.runFrom`, marked "this `match`" above; the compiler turned it into a function that source can't name. The same `match` written again is another such function, equal to it only if neither calls the definition it's written in. To state a lemma about it, make it a local `def` in its arm and refer to it as `owner.name`, where `owner` is the definition it is written in (docs/syntax.md, "Proving things about a definition with an inner `match`")
─────╯
